Valerie,

After I got your last message, I gave a call to Don Kates.  He is
one of the nation's pre-eminent authorities on gun control (He is
the author of several books including, /Gun Control: The Liberal
Skeptics Speak Out/).  I asked him to clarify the "verifiable
threats" quirk I previously mentioned to you.

In essence, you can legally carry, concealed or otherwise, if you
have a legitimately obtained restraining order against someone
who has physically threatened you, *and* the threat is currently
realistic.  In other words, if you know the person threatening
you is in jail, or some such, you may not carry until such time
as the threat again becomes viable.  Of course, the restraining
order must be in effect at that time, too.

Now for the bad news.  Even if you have a restraining order and
the person name is a current threat, you still may run afoul of
the law.  If (and that's a big "if") a cop catches you with a
weapon, he will arrest first and ask questions later ("tell it to
the judge").  What could easily happen is:  a) You would be
arrested and your gun would be seized.  b) The DA would offer you
a deal whereby he would cut you loose, but would not let you have
your gun back.  This is the kind of deal DAs often cut.  It often
works because most people don't have the money to fight the
charges.  Be advised though, the law does allow you to carry
under the circumstances.  You would win if you were willing to
pursue the matter in court.  In practice, though, cops and DAs
violate rights in this way, all the time.  Makes me want to puke.

Should you ever find yourself threatened by someone, your best
bet it to get a restraining order and then go ahead and carry.
Unless you are very careless or unlucky, no one will ever know.
On the off chance your carry is discovered, and you can't afford
to fight city hall, take the deal.  Let them have your gun in
exchange for dropping all charges.  Then go out an get another
gun.  (Less expensive guns aren't a bad idea under this sort of
circumstance.)

