Date: Mon, 17 Jan 1994 12:43:21 -0800
From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Subject: guns: legal carry

In CA, you may not carry a loaded weapon, concealed or otherwise,
without a specific permit.  Without such a permit, the CA firearms
laws (handguns) are very explicit, being, roughly:

1.  Weapon uloaded.  Clip out.
2.  Weapon in locked case.  No ammo or clips in case.
3.  Clips unloaded.

Several of these items are currently under dispute, are not worded
very clearly, and are interpreted differently by different people
(e.g. the cop that stops and searches you).  I *strongly* suggest you
get a copy of the CA 1993 gun laws (about a $3 pamphlet at most sport
shops).  Otherwise, contact your local law enforcement agency and ask
specific questions.

Off the record, most people I have talked to (including cops) say:
"Don't get caught".  I think you have to personally assess the relative
risks between getting caught with a weapon and not having one if you
need it.  Note that conviction for a weapon violation may preclude you
from legally owning a weapon ever again.  If you know you're going into
a dangerous place/circumstance: don't go.

However, I'm sure that many people (other than crooks) do carry
weapons.  Every time I see someone with a small hip pack I wonder: "is
this person packing?".  Personally, I support responsible firearms
ownership and use --including concealed carry-- and believe that the
laws should be changed.  Probably the best thing you can do to start
this process is to *GET DIANNE FEINSTEIN OUT OF THE SENATE*.  One half
of *your* state representation in this part of Congress is thoroughly
and vocally against you all the way on this issue.  Do something about
it. 

--Vince

