Saw an interesting idea in a programme about the recent temporary
extinction (we _are_ going to clone them from salvaged DNA, aren't we?)
of the giant mammoth.  They were suggesting that a contributory (perhaps
major) factor in the extinction of mammoths and other large animals was
human predation (eg by the Clovis people) in the immediate post ice-age. 
As early modern man invaded (er, colonised) Siberia, N.America,
Australia and Europe they wiped out all large indigenous animals (giant
ground sloths, mammoths, saber-tooths...).

But in Africa (and some parts of Asia?) animals had co-evolved with
early hominids over millions of years and had time to evolve defence
strategies against spears, rocks and pits.  The programme didn't say
what these adaptions were, but I imagine that running away from bipeds
is high on the list.

