Date: Mon, 17 Jan 94 16:48:11 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Bring back the wooly mammoth!

There was an extinction of more than 50 large mammal species in North
America at the end of the Pleistocene, which was indeed approximately
the time of arrival of the first wave of human colonists (on foot).

Some of the adaptations of animals to human predators seem to be cultural
rather than genetic.  A book on management of African wildlife which I
once read (reference at home) cited work on rhinoceros response to humans.
In areas where rhinos have primarily been hunted with short-range weapons,
they charge -- evidently to scatter the hunters before they can bring
weapons into effective use.  In areas where there has been much hunting
with rifles -- which certainly hasn't been for long enough for significant
rhino evolution to occur -- they run away rapidly on seeing humans.  And in
areas where they have been hunted little or not at all -- there still were
a few of these in the 1960s, when the work in question was done -- rhinos
are quite docile.

                                          -- Jay Freeman

