Tim, thanks for your reply. I'm aware that it is standard to assume that if 
one person is offended, it is the other person's fault (in local, middle-to-
upper class interpersonal relations) and that some people just won't respect
other people's preferences with regard to privacy. However, I think it is
important to recognize that people have different preferences with regard
to the degree of privacy they prefer, and that, since people generally come
to us with very poor documentation, we have to guess. It is true that it is
standard and usually considered polite in local, middle-to-
upper class interpersonal relations to guess conservatively, but it is also
true that the way people get close to each other is to take incremental risks
that don't conform to that standard. There are some kinds of people I want
to know, and I appreciate it when they take privacy-corrupting risks of this
kind with me. There are some kinds of people I don't want to know (too many,
I sometimes think) and I do not appreciate the same behavior in them. But
it is impossible for anyone to be 'sensitive' enough to know what I will
appreciate before they try. The trick is to use the right size increments,
which standard also varies.

