>The more ideological baggage I can shed, the better I feel. I come to this
>list for discussion of interesting topics, not to find new blinders.

Amen^H^H^H^H...ah...er...I mean, I agree completely, unless you mean to say
that love and sex aren't interesting topics. <nudge nudge wink wink, you
know what I mean?>

I too strongly advocate "meme garbage-collection". We can improve our lives
and ourselves by re-evaluating all of the memes and values we have
collected over the years and tossing out the irrational and the ones that
don't work for us (as individuals). It often works best if we get a chance
to discuss them with other like-minded individuals.

In this case I was encouraged (by Vince) to evaluate a relationship based
on a certain framework, the principles (which are written, btw, as mallable
guidelines, not as commandments). Upon doing the evaluation I discovered
that I hadn't stressed the motives and goals enough when discussing an
"extropian" (perhaps transhuman would be a better term?) relationship. A
useful exercise, if you ask me.

IMHEO (that's Humbly Extropian), the only time a relationship is
un-extropian is when you stop thinking about what you are doing and let the
other partner(s) or society (which would include an extropian society)
define your relationship and/or your role in a relationship. Is that too
restricting?

PS - Unless you (Kathy or anyone else) have a strong urge to discuss this
in public, please follow-up in private e-mail. I doubt the list-population
is very interested in another "Extropianism is turning into a religion"
thread. Note that I have a high opinion of both Kathy and her opinions, I
just want to avoid moving forward on this (potentially noisy) thread if we
don't have to.

