Most of you are already aware of the special circumstance I
covered in e-mail to Amara.  Beyond that, though, it is my
understanding that it is only illegal to carry loaded (but not
concealed) within municipal limits.  In unincorporated areas, it
is perfectly legal to carry a loaded gun on your hip.  Now, as a
practical matter, cops tend to draw down on you under such
circumstances unless you really look like a deer hunter or
something.  This also happens if you are openly carrying an
unloaded gun within city limits.

There is one interesting quirk to the carry laws.  A gun is
considered loaded if the person carrying it has ammunition on his
person *and* is within a certain number of blocks of the State
Assembly in Sacramento.  (Those guys *hate* armed taxpayers!)

Vince also wrote:

    Note that conviction for a weapon violation may preclude
    you from legally owning a weapon ever again.

I suppose some weapons violations could have that effect, but
generally, a weapons convictions is to be avoided because it
makes any *subsequent* gun violations into felonies.  (Carrying a
concealed knife is a felony.  Carrying a concealed gun is a
misdemeanor *unless* you have a previous weapons conviction.)


